1. Summary of Li et al. (2025) and Its Supplement
Li et al. prepared Co(x)Fe({3-x})*O(4) hollow-sphere absorbers (monodisperse, 0≤x≤1) to study how Co(^{2+}) content affects microwave absorption. They measured complex permittivity (\varepsilon(f)) and permeability (\mu(f)), and used standard transmission-line theory to compute reflection loss (RL) versus frequency. For each sample, the minimum RL ((RL{\min})) occurs at a particular frequency and sample thickness (Table S3). For example, one sample (nominally CoFe(_2)O(_4), x≈1) shows an especially deep minimum of about –72.3 dB, indicating very strong absorption.

In the Supplementary Material, the authors plot real and imaginary parts of (\varepsilon) and (\mu) for each sample. They also compute the input impedance (Z_{\rm in}) (Figure S9), presumably via
[ Z_{\rm in}(f) = Z_0,\sqrt{\frac{\mu(f)}{\varepsilon(f)}},\tanh!\bigl[j,k_0 d\sqrt{\varepsilon(f)\mu(f)},\bigr], ] where (d) is thickness, (k_0=2\pi f/c), and (Z_0=377,\Omega). From (Z_{\rm in}) they can calculate RL via (20\log_{10}|(Z_{\rm in}-Z_0)/(Z_{\rm in}+Z_0)|). Although the main text does not show the raw impedance data, it emphasizes phase relationships: Figure S10 plots the phase of the front-reflected wave (beam “r”) and of the back-reflected wave (“t”) as functions of frequency. The authors note that “(RL_{\min}) for all samples occurred when the phase difference between beams t and r was approximately (\pi)”, i.e. the two reflected waves are out of phase.

Li et al. explicitly cite the wave-interference theory to interpret this. The quoted text in the main paper reads (with Liu et al. references [65–66] for the theory, and [32,67] for verification):

“…which can be explained through the phase cancellation model in the wave cancellation theory (Fig. S10(a) and S10(b)) [65,66]. These results are in good agreement with those of the wave cancellation theory [32,67].”

Here “wave cancellation theory” refers to the analysis developed by Liu et al. (2021–2023) that treats the film as a multi-interface system and attributes RL minima to destructive interference of reflected waves. By citing this, Li et al. acknowledge the relevance of wave interference. However, they do not make the bold claim that the conventional impedance-matching mechanism is wrong. Instead, they describe the wave-based interpretation as “in good agreement” with the observations. Their wording suggests coexistence of explanations rather than a refutation of one.

In summary, the paper’s contribution is to show that by varying composition, the measured RL spectra can be modeled by both the conventional formulas and by considering wave phase cancellation. The main text still uses language of materials and structure affecting absorption, but it implicitly links the observed peaks to interface-phase effects. Importantly, the authors do not present any numerical contradiction in the equations: they simply replace some figures with wave-based plots. Thus, while the paper does introduce wave concepts, it stops short of explicitly overturning the older theory.

2. Impedance-Matching vs. Wave-Mechanics: Equations and Calculations
To analyze these theories, we reconstruct the key formulas.

2.1 Impedance-Matching Theory
The classical model treats the absorber film as a uniform slab backed by a perfect conductor (metal backing). The input impedance (Z_{\rm in}) of the film seen from air is [ Z_{\rm in} = Z_0,\sqrt{\frac{\mu_r}{\varepsilon_r}};\tanh!\bigl[j,k_0 d\sqrt{\varepsilon_r\mu_r},\bigr], ] where (\varepsilon_r(f)) and (\mu_r(f)) are the complex relative permittivity/permeability, (k_0=2\pi f/c), (d) is thickness, and (Z_0=377,\Omega). Reflection loss (dB) is then computed by the transmission-line formula: [ RL = 20\log_{10}\Bigl|\frac{Z_{\rm in} - Z_0}{Z_{\rm in} + Z_0}\Bigr|. ] In impedance-matching theory, the minimum of (RL) occurs when the film’s input impedance matches free space ((Z_{\rm in}=Z_0)), so that no reflection occurs. In practice one often invokes a quarter-wavelength condition: the thickness is such that (d = (2n+1),\lambda/4) inside the material (where (\lambda=2\pi/k_0\sqrt{\varepsilon_r\mu_r}) is the wave wavelength in the film). However, recent work has shown that attributing RL minima to a “resonance” inside the material is flawed. The mathematical derivation in Liu et al. (2023) proves that the film’s RL minima are not due to a simple impedance match of material resonance peaks, but arise from interface interference (see below).

2.2 Wave Mechanics (Phase Interference) Model
The wave mechanics or interference model explicitly tracks the two reflected beams: the front-face reflection (r_1) and the back-face reflection (r_2). For a metal-backed film, the back reflection has a phase flip of π. One can derive (using transfer-matrix methods) that the total reflection coefficient (r_{\rm tot}) seen in amplitude is the sum of multiple contributions. In the simplest single-bounce approximation: [ r_{\rm tot} \approx r_1 + t_1^2(-e^{-2j\beta d}), ] where (\beta = k_0\sqrt{\varepsilon_r\mu_r}), (t_1 = 2Z_0/(Z_0+Z_{\rm in})) is the front-face transmission, and (-e^{-2j\beta d}) is the phase factor from one round trip (π flip at metal plus propagation (2\beta d)). More generally, summing infinite bounces gives the exact result, but the key point is:

The phase difference between (r_1) and the returning wave (r_2\approx -e^{-2j\beta d}) determines interference. When this phase difference is ~π, the two reflections cancel, yielding a deep RL minimum.

Physically, this occurs when (2\beta d \approx 2\pi n) (so that (r_2=-1\cdot (+1) = -1)), making (r_2 \approx +1) times (-e^{-2j\beta d}), which cancels (r_1) in amplitude.

In practice, one computes the same (Z_{\rm in}) as before, but one can also compute and plot (r_1) and (r_2) as vectors on the complex plane (or on a Smith chart). The RL (in dB) is (20\log_{10}|r_{\rm tot}|). An RL curve computed via wave interference will match the transmission-line result if done correctly, but the interpretation of how the minimum occurs is different: it is due to phase cancellation, not material resonance.

2.3 Calculational Example and Code
The essential calculation steps are: obtain (\varepsilon_r(f)), (\mu_r(f)) from measurements; then for each frequency:

Compute (Z_{\rm in} = Z_0\sqrt{\mu_r/\varepsilon_r}\tanh[j,\beta d]).

Compute front reflection (r_1 = (Z_{\rm in} - Z_0)/(Z_{\rm in} + Z_0)).

Compute back reflection component (r_2 = -,e^{-2j\beta d}) (amplitude of 1 into metal, minus sign for PEC).

Sum (r_{\rm tot} = r_1 + t_1^2(-e^{-2j\beta d})), where (t_1 = 2Z_0/(Z_{\rm in}+Z_0)).

Compute (RL = 20\log_{10}|r_{\rm tot}|).

