3. Theoretical Competence Demonstrated by the Paper
The Li et al. paper shows that the authors can perform the basic calculations: they have computed (|Z_{\rm in}|) (Fig. S9) and the phase of reflections (Fig. S10). They identify the condition (|Z_{\rm in}|\approx Z_0) and phase difference ≈π at the absorption peak, which is consistent with theory. In particular, they note that for CoFe(_2)O(4) the mismatch between (|R_M(d^-)|) and (|V_t/V_i|) is only 0.0002, yielding RL({\min}=-72.3) dB (Table S3) – i.e. the impedance condition is tightly met.

By comparing the data to both the conventional model and the Liu–Drew wave-cancellation model (via Figs. S9–S10), the paper does provide evidence that the wave approach fits the experiment “better.” In effect, Li et al. have the minimal ability to check these theories: they calculate phase shifts and confirm when (\varphi_t-\varphi_r\approx\pi) (as the wave theory predicts) and when (|Z_{\rm in}| \approx Z_0). These are indeed necessary checks.

However, the paper does not fully exploit this capability. Crucially, the authors do not conclude that the conventional theory is wrong; they merely say the data “can be explained” by wave cancellation. They do not explicitly identify any flaw or inconsistency in the impedance-matching formulas. For example, they do not point out that, under wave theory, there can be only one RL minimum (at (\varphi\approx\pi)) for a given film thickness, whereas classical quarter-wave theory might predict two symmetric lobes. Nor do they show a case where the impedance criterion fails even though an RL peak exists. In short, they do not perform the scientific test of showing which theory’s assumptions break down.

Thus, while Li et al. have demonstrated the basic calculations required to compare theories, they do so only to verify that the interference condition occurs. They have identified phase cancellation in their data, but they have not turned their tools around to challenge the older explanation. This reflects what Yue Liu calls “theoretical poverty”: one can compute with an accepted model without truly understanding or questioning it. The Li paper cites the wave-theory references (showing awareness) but stops at observing agreement, rather than engaging with the mathematical argument.

4. Fitting the Data vs. Explaining the Mechanism
It is important to distinguish curve fitting from mechanistic explanation. Li et al. find that their measurements agree with the predictions of wave-cancellation theory. This means the same data can be reproduced by plugging the measured (\varepsilon,\mu) into either set of formulas. Indeed, for a symmetric film on metal, the impedance formula and the interference formula yield identical numerical values for RL (since one is derived from the other). So agreement alone does not prove one mechanism is right and the other wrong.

What the wave mechanics theory argues is that the cause of the absorption minimum is fundamentally the cancellation of reflected waves, not the material’s impedance reaching an “intrinsic resonance.” J. Appl. Phys. 134, 045303 (2023) (Liu et al.) demonstrates this rigorously: it proves that for a backed film the RL minimum arises only when the reflected-beam phases align for cancellation, regardless of how (\varepsilon) and (\mu) vary. In that study, the authors show that even if the material has no intrinsic resonance at that frequency, the film’s thickness can force destructive interference. Thus the phase condition is sufficient and primary. The same paper also quantifies “the flaws of neglecting phase effects” in the conventional theory.

Therefore, while Li et al. correctly observe that their data “agree with” the wave model, one cannot simply conclude from this that impedance-matching theory is equivalent or correct. In fact, if the wave-based derivation is correct, continuing to apply the old interpretation is meaningless. As noted in [28], “If the wave-mechanics derivation is correct, then the continued repetition of impedance matching cannot make the old explanation correct”. The true test is to show which derivation survives logical scrutiny.

In practical terms, predictive success demands checking specific consequences: for example, verifying that only a single RL minimum occurs per half-wavelength of film thickness (as wave theory predicts) instead of the two resonances expected under quarter-wave theory. Another check would be to show that even with (|Z_{\rm in}|\neq Z_0), RL can dip if phase is tuned, which would directly contradict impedance-matching emphasis. These are the kinds of tests Li et al. do not report. Instead, they acknowledge the wave perspective as “an explanation” rather than forcing a confrontation.

