The editorial and revision history of the paper sheds light on the reluctance to renounce the conventional view. According to public commentary, Li et al.’s initial manuscript used only the conventional impedance approach and did not consider wave interference. Only after peer review did a referee point out the Liu–Drew work on wave-cancellation theory, prompting the authors to revise and incorporate the new analysis. This story (while based on second-hand reports) suggests that awareness of wave mechanics in the field is spreading, but acceptance remains cautious.

As for transparency: the published paper and its supplement include the computed curves for permittivity, permeability, impedance and RL. However, raw numeric data are not provided. The calculations leading to Figures S9–S10 rely on the measured (\varepsilon(f),\mu(f)), but those are only shown graphically. If one wanted to reproduce the results, one would have to digitize these plots. No open repository or digital tables of the data are cited. In modern best practice, depositing the measured complex permittivity and permeability would allow independent testing of both theories. As of now, this data availability is limited to the published graphs.

Regarding reviewer comments or editorial correspondence: such information is normally confidential and is not publicly posted. We have only the authors’ second-hand account. There is no indication that the journal requested or enforced open data. In summary, the paper’s transparency is typical of many experimental reports: it shares key results but not detailed data sets. Crucially, the authors do not publish any analysis code or explicit derivations (in contrast to the wave-theory papers, which include full math). Readers must therefore trust the authors’ computations of (Z_{\rm in}) and phase. Any hypothetical critique would require the same effort: digitize the plots and re-run the formulas.

6. Further Tests: Calculations and Code
To definitively compare theories, one should independently compute RL for the given samples and see which conditions govern the minimum. Key steps would include:

Acquire or extract material parameters: Obtain the complex (\varepsilon(f)) and (\mu(f)) for each sample. If raw data are not available, digitize the published curves (e.g. using graphical software).

Compute RL vs. frequency and thickness: Using the formulas from §2, calculate RL for the thicknesses studied. Plot these against the experimental RL to check quantitative agreement.

Locate minima and their causes: For each RL minimum, check whether (|Z_{\rm in}|\approx Z_0) and whether the phase difference (\Delta\varphi\approx\pi). A code snippet in Python might look like:

